Let the integral be \(I\).
I = \int_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4} \frac{x + \frac{\pi}{4}}{2 - \cos 2x} dx
We can split the integral into two parts:
I = \int_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4} \frac{xdx}{2 - \cos 2x} + \int_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4} \frac{\frac{\pi}{4}dx}{2 - \cos 2x}
For the first integral, let \(f(x) = \frac{x}{2 - \cos 2x}\). Since \(\cos(-2x) = \cos(2x)\), we have \(f(-x) = \frac{-x}{2 - \cos 2x} = -f(x)\). Thus, \(f(x)\) is an odd function. The integral of an odd function over a symmetric interval \([-a, a]\) is 0.
\int_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4} \frac{xdx}{2 - \cos 2x} = 0
For the second integral, let \(g(x) = \frac{1}{2 - \cos 2x}\). Since \(g(-x) = \frac{1}{2 - \cos(-2x)} = \frac{1}{2 - \cos 2x} = g(x)\), \(g(x)\) is an even function. The integral of an even function over a symmetric interval \([-a, a]\) is \(2 \int_{0}^{a} g(x) dx\).
I = 0 + \frac{\pi}{4} \cdot 2 \int_{0}^{\pi/4} \frac{dx}{2 - \cos 2x}
Using the identity \(\cos 2x = 1 - 2\sin^2 x\):
I = \frac{\pi}{2} \int_{0}^{\pi/4} \frac{dx}{2 - (1 - 2\sin^2 x)}
I = \frac{\pi}{2} \int_{0}^{\pi/4} \frac{dx}{1 + 2\sin^2 x}
Divide the numerator and denominator by \(\cos^2 x\):
I = \frac{\pi}{2} \int_{0}^{\pi/4} \frac{\sec^2 x dx}{\frac{1}{\cos^2 x} + \frac{2\sin^2 x}{\cos^2 x}}
I = \frac{\pi}{2} \int_{0}^{\pi/4} \frac{\sec^2 x dx}{\sec^2 x + 2\tan^2 x}
Substitute \(\sec^2 x = 1 + \tan^2 x\) in the denominator:
I = \frac{\pi}{2} \int_{0}^{\pi/4} \frac{\sec^2 x dx}{(1 + \tan^2 x) + 2\tan^2 x}
I = \frac{\pi}{2} \int_{0}^{\pi/4} \frac{\sec^2 x dx}{1 + 3\tan^2 x}
Now, let \(t = \tan x\). Then, \(dt = \sec^2 x dx\).
Change the limits of integration:
• When \(x = 0\), \(t = \tan 0 = 0\).
• When \(x = \pi/4\), \(t = \tan(\pi/4) = 1\).
I = \frac{\pi}{2} \int_{0}^{1} \frac{dt}{1 + 3t^2}
This is a standard integral of the form \(\int \frac{dx}{a^2 + (bx)^2} = \frac{1}{ab} \tan^{-1}(\frac{bx}{a})\). Here, \(a=1\), \(b=\sqrt{3}\).
I = \frac{\pi}{2} \left[ \frac{1}{\sqrt{3}} \tan^{-1}(\sqrt{3}t) \right]_{0}^{1}
I = \frac{\pi}{2\sqrt{3}} \left[ \tan^{-1}(\sqrt{3} \cdot 1) - \tan^{-1}(\sqrt{3} \cdot 0) \right]
I = \frac{\pi}{2\sqrt{3}} \left[ \tan^{-1}(\sqrt{3}) - \tan^{-1}(0) \right]
I = \frac{\pi}{2\sqrt{3}} \left[ \frac{\pi}{3} - 0 \right]
I = \frac{\pi}{2\sqrt{3}} \cdot \frac{\pi}{3}
I = \frac{\pi^2}{6\sqrt{3}}

